From 53996162027911d7edf71339623f8e9a45d638e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rautshivamxyz-beep <rautshivamxyz@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 04:01:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] v9.33.153 "An append that does not copy the file"

v9.33.151 left three bare File.appendText calls in the app, and said so in the
code: the notification log, the auto-reply log and the wiki store. All three
had the same measured reason. NcieFiles.append is crash-safe precisely because
it reads the whole file and writes it back, which makes it O(size) per call -
and these three are appended to once per notification (a 512 KB cap), once per
auto-reply (60 KB), and once per stored wiki article in a store that is
several megabytes and indexed by byte offset. Routing them through append()
would have rewritten half a megabyte to add one line, on the thread that had
just received a notification. That cost is why the exceptions existed.

So the one save gets the form those call sites actually need. appendFast takes
the same per-path lock - two threads can no longer interleave their bytes into
one torn line - and refuses a file it cannot read, so it still will not append
a second half onto a damaged whole. What it does not do is write atomically: a
kill mid-append can leave a partial line. That is the trade a log makes and a
state file must not, and the code says so, so the next reader does not reach
for it on a store.

With that, the last three bare appends are gone and the rule is absolute: every
write in NOVA goes through the one save. Two things follow that were not
possible before - the wiki store's append now takes the lock every other save
takes (it was the one multi-megabyte write with no lock at all), and a
notification log that cannot be read is left alone instead of being appended
onto.

The lock count is tied to the number of entry points, not to a number, so a
fourth one cannot silently skip it (the v9.33.124 rule). The cost is pinned by
a unit test rather than asserted in prose: a 1 MB log is appended to, and every
byte of it is still there afterwards with the new line after it.
